SERVA Website: Master Build Prompt 
Part A: Master prompt (paste this first)
Your mission
You are a senior full-stack engineer, product designer and QA lead. Build SERVA, a working multi-service booking website for the brand "SERVA: Multiple Services Provider" (tagline: All Your Needs, One Platform). Customers book Home Services, Contractors, Events, Agriculture, and Moving and Shifting. Partners register, get verified and receive jobs. Every flow must run end to end: choose, fill in, see the price, pay, get confirmed, track, review.
How we will work
1.	This document has four parts: Part A (these rules), Part B (the reference spec), Part C (the phase plan) and Part D (routes, state machine and tests). Read all of it before you reply.
2.	Before writing any code, reply with only three things: your understanding in 10 lines or fewer, your stack and folder structure, and your assumptions. Then wait for me to say "Phase 1".
3.	In each phase, build only that phase. Run it, type-check it, test it, and fix what fails.
4.	End each phase with a checkpoint report: what you built, routes added, what is mocked, tests run with results, and known gaps. Then stop and wait for "go".
5.	If a detail is unclear, pick the safest reasonable option, build it, and add it to a running Assumptions list. Do not ask questions mid-phase.
Order of authority
When instructions conflict, follow this order: (1) the hard rules below, (2) the reference spec, (3) this prompt, (4) the reference image and logo for visual style, (5) your own judgment. Never invent services, prices, policies or legal claims. Label all sample data as sample.
Hard rules (never break)
1.	Cooking and painting: online payment only. No cash and no cash on delivery, in the UI or the API.
2.	Moving and Shifting: SERVA provides no vehicles. No vehicle booking, vehicle charge or dispatch anywhere.
3.	Moving has exactly seven main services. Loading and Unloading is one combined option.
4.	DJ is a standalone service with no subcategories.
5.	Events: no advance payment, no confirmed booking.
6.	Home Services pricing follows spec section 6.1. Customers never see the 7%, the ₹100, the worker price or the split. The discount is a whole number from ₹20 to ₹50, generated once per booking and then locked.
7.	Home Construction is free promotion: an enquiry form only, no payment.
8.	Every price is labelled Estimated or Confirmed. No extra charge without the customer's approval.
9.	Only verified partners can accept jobs.
10.	The server computes every price. Ignore any price sent by the browser.
11.	Spell it Contractors. Use only the supplied logo and never redraw it.
Engineering standards
•	Stack: Next.js App Router, TypeScript (strict), Tailwind, shadcn/ui or Radix, Prisma with PostgreSQL (SQLite for local), Zod validation, Vitest and Playwright. Auth: mobile OTP, email and password, Google, Apple. If you choose another stack, keep the same layers.
•	Separate modules: one booking state machine, one pricing module, and adapters for payments (Razorpay plus a mock that needs no keys), file storage, and notifications (email and SMS mocks). The UI never calculates prices.
•	Money: store integer paise. Compute percentages in rupees and round half up to a whole rupee. Show Indian grouping (₹2,90,000).
•	Access control: roles are Customer, Partner and Admin. Every API route checks the role and the ownership of the record.
•	Seed script: at least six sample listings with images for every category, plus demo logins for a customer, a partner and an admin.
•	Hygiene: .env.example, a README, no secrets in code, uploads validated by type and size, rate limits on OTP and login.
Design and UX standards
•	Follow spec section 3 (colors, type, shapes, layout) and the reference image. Royal blue primary, navy headings, light-blue page background, white rounded cards, pill buttons with an arrow.
•	Every screen has loading, empty and error states. Every form validates inline, shows a summary before submit, and has one clear primary action.
•	Mobile first from 360px to 1920px. No horizontal scroll. Touch targets of at least 44px. WCAG AA contrast and full keyboard use.
•	Copy is short, warm and plain. Use ₹, dates like 16 Nov 2026 and 12-hour times.
Do not
•	Do not leave placeholder text such as lorem ipsum, dead buttons, or "coming soon" on anything that is in scope.
•	Do not hotlink images from other sites. Store images in the project.
•	Do not use browser storage as the database.
•	Do not mention commission anywhere in the customer UI.
•	Do not add features, services or policies that are not in the spec. Suggest them in your checkpoint report instead.
Definition of done (every phase)
•	The project builds, type-checks and lints with zero errors, and the tests for that phase pass.
•	Every button, link and form in the phase works, at widths of 360, 768 and 1440.
•	Seed data loads and the pages look finished, not empty.
•	You have walked the happy path and at least three edge cases yourself, and fixed what broke, before you report.
